  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on a specific calculation: an aspiration level derived from the mean of all outcomes, followed by evaluating which lottery has a higher probability of exceeding this level. The aspiration level (~-2333.33) serves as a reference point, and only outcomes above it (like 0 in Lottery B) count as positive differences. The individual’s reasoning aligns with the conclusion (choosing B due to the chance of 0) but does not reference any calculation of an average outcome or a systematic comparison to a computed aspiration level. Instead, the justification is more intuitive: focusing on the presence of a no-loss outcome and similarity in loss magnitude. While the individual values the same favorable outcome (0 Euros) that the reason identifies as 'positive', they do not appear to derive this from the formal aspiration-level rule. The core mechanism of the reason—calculating a mean and assessing deviations—is absent in the report. Therefore, while there is partial alignment in outcome focus, the reasoning process does not match the structure of the decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 40
